00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:11,520
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Kerrio.

6
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:11,861
[SPEAKER_07]: Present.

7
00:00:13,422 --> 00:00:14,122
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Collins.

8
00:00:14,963 --> 00:00:15,283
[SPEAKER_06]: Present.

9
00:00:15,664 --> 00:00:16,224
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Knight.

10
00:00:19,126 --> 00:00:19,947
[SPEAKER_07]: I don't see Councilor Knight.

11
00:00:19,987 --> 00:00:20,588
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Scarpelli.

12
00:00:20,608 --> 00:00:20,788
[SPEAKER_07]: Present.

13
00:00:22,909 --> 00:00:23,530
[SPEAKER_07]: Councilor Sang.

14
00:00:24,351 --> 00:00:24,731
[SPEAKER_07]: Present.

15
00:00:25,151 --> 00:00:25,672
[SPEAKER_07]: President Morrell.

16
00:00:26,534 --> 00:00:35,599
[SPEAKER_06]: As a six present one absent the meeting is called to order, there will be a meeting of the Medford City Council to the whole on Tuesday, May 24 2022 at 545pm.

17
00:00:36,099 --> 00:00:41,682
[SPEAKER_06]: The purpose of this meeting is to discuss and compile proposals and ideas from the city council for the FY 23 budget to 2-354.

18
00:00:43,653 --> 00:00:48,675
[SPEAKER_06]: For further information, aids and accommodations, contact the city clerk at 781-393-2425.

19
00:00:49,156 --> 00:00:51,116
[SPEAKER_06]: Sincerely yours, Nicole Morel, council president.

20
00:00:51,877 --> 00:01:02,362
[SPEAKER_06]: So as we discussed last week, the intention of this meeting is to allow the council to share ideas as far as what we would like to see as part of this year's, the fiscal year 23 budget.

21
00:01:02,942 --> 00:01:07,524
[SPEAKER_06]: There had been hopes in past years of having pre-budget meetings with the administration.

22
00:01:07,624 --> 00:01:09,525
[SPEAKER_06]: Unfortunately, that hasn't come to pass.

23
00:01:10,185 --> 00:01:21,951
[SPEAKER_06]: So the intent of this meeting is to at least gather ideas from the council, see if we can come to some agreement or a number of ideas that we endorse and then send the paper to the administration, hopefully for consideration as they finalize the budget.

24
00:01:22,051 --> 00:01:30,776
[SPEAKER_06]: So we do have a few papers that some councilors submitted in advance as far as ideas, and we can also just go around and hear what councilors wanna include.

25
00:01:31,797 --> 00:01:32,557
[SPEAKER_06]: Anyone would like to start?

26
00:01:33,337 --> 00:01:34,018
[SPEAKER_06]: Vice President Behrs.

27
00:01:34,345 --> 00:01:35,326
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Madam President.

28
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:38,168
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm glad that we're holding this meeting.

29
00:01:38,188 --> 00:01:46,296
[SPEAKER_03]: I think it is good for this council to initiate the budget process.

30
00:01:46,756 --> 00:01:50,900
[SPEAKER_03]: In a sense, before we receive a budget that's already been baked in.

31
00:01:51,441 --> 00:01:53,342
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think that's, that's one of the goals here.

32
00:01:54,925 --> 00:02:02,373
[SPEAKER_03]: I know that we come up every year with our list of Council priorities, some of them are budgetary some of them are legislative some of them are a combination of the two.

33
00:02:03,675 --> 00:02:08,660
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think you know, to some extent, some of this discussion may be an extension of those previous discussions.

34
00:02:12,157 --> 00:02:34,675
[SPEAKER_03]: in what I brought forward today here was to try to find from the discussions that we've had a few consensus items that all of us agree on with the intent of really saying to the administration very directly that this, you know, all seven of us want these things and they have to be in the budget.

35
00:02:36,455 --> 00:02:42,161
[SPEAKER_03]: In a short summary, and I'll go through it in more specifics or happy to answer questions from my fellow counselors.

36
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:57,416
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the funding for our phase two of zoning, which we still have not received, which I think we all really would like to get going on as we now have many plans coming to fruition that will need to have significant zoning work to make them actionable.

37
00:02:58,757 --> 00:03:03,020
[SPEAKER_03]: One of them is a code enforcement officer, which I know we've all spoken extensively about.

38
00:03:03,501 --> 00:03:07,744
[SPEAKER_03]: Councilor Caravaglio had a resolution recently about it, I believe.

39
00:03:09,306 --> 00:03:11,487
[SPEAKER_03]: First to last, so all the years.

40
00:03:14,555 --> 00:03:26,780
[SPEAKER_03]: just how much in this community comes down to code enforcement and how many the quality of life issues that residents want to be addressed really comes down to making sure that the codes are enforced.

41
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,060
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's in here as well.

42
00:03:29,521 --> 00:03:40,145
[SPEAKER_03]: We created an elections department, but that department still does not have a, we have the elections coordinator, Gail, obviously in the position that she's been in for several months now.

43
00:03:40,785 --> 00:03:43,948
[SPEAKER_03]: But there has not been a posting for the director of elections position.

44
00:03:45,289 --> 00:03:47,111
[SPEAKER_03]: It has not been put out there.

45
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:54,136
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a department head now I mean it's managing a full department managing full election so that needs to be addressed in this budget.

46
00:03:55,358 --> 00:03:57,659
[SPEAKER_03]: And then of course, we have here.

47
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:05,072
[SPEAKER_03]: bringing an assistant city solicitor back to the city and making sure that we have our legal counsel in house.

48
00:04:05,132 --> 00:04:13,935
[SPEAKER_03]: I think we've all seen the impact of the current arrangement on our law department, on the ability of this council to do its work.

49
00:04:15,215 --> 00:04:25,999
[SPEAKER_03]: And quite frankly, on some less than collaborative legal work and opinions that have come to us relatively recently that I find,

50
00:04:27,139 --> 00:04:28,880
[SPEAKER_03]: a little concerning to say the least.

51
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:32,642
[SPEAKER_03]: So those were the intentions here of what I put forward.

52
00:04:33,723 --> 00:04:40,986
[SPEAKER_03]: More specifically, you can see that I did pull line items and tried to account for the budget impact of these requests.

53
00:04:42,287 --> 00:04:45,489
[SPEAKER_03]: They're based on the fiscal 22 budget.

54
00:04:45,709 --> 00:04:54,514
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm sure that there may be proposed adjustments to line items, you know, perhaps changes in salaries,

55
00:04:56,318 --> 00:05:02,383
[SPEAKER_03]: with new contracts or with potential raises for non-union employees who are in the CAF system.

56
00:05:03,184 --> 00:05:12,371
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you know, I'm not married to any specific number necessarily, but I wanted to try to come to a reasonable accounting of these different ideas.

57
00:05:12,491 --> 00:05:17,856
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you know, you can see that the 50,000 for zoning, that's pretty clear, that's a $50,000 budget impact.

58
00:05:20,222 --> 00:05:29,004
[SPEAKER_03]: Code enforcement I looked at the B 5.5 pay scale and step one was around 84,000 so that's about the budget impact of that.

59
00:05:30,378 --> 00:05:38,784
[SPEAKER_03]: elections department I looked at comparable positions across the area and there's kind of a range from around $85,000 to $118,000 for people who run elections departments and neighboring communities.

60
00:05:38,824 --> 00:05:44,247
[SPEAKER_03]: So I I looked at calf 16 which runs from about 87 to $97,000 as what I would recommend for that position.

61
00:05:51,923 --> 00:06:17,342
[SPEAKER_03]: um and then a total budget impact there of about 70 to 80 thousand dollars and then in the legal department looked at the fiscal year 20 and 21 budgets where there was an assistant city solicitor uh budgeted for um and I you know put that back into the budget and I reduced the contract legal services line item which is currently paying outside council from eighty one thousand six hundred to twenty five thousand dollars

62
00:06:18,182 --> 00:06:21,164
[SPEAKER_03]: So bringing back an assistant city solicitor would only cost $2,000.

63
00:06:22,045 --> 00:06:26,248
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think that's more than reasonable.

64
00:06:26,268 --> 00:06:39,917
[SPEAKER_03]: I think all of these are more than reasonable requests, but my goal with these proposals, and I also recognize that President Morrell, you have many similar proposals, is that we could all vote for them and really send a message to the mayor that these have to be in the budget.

65
00:06:41,118 --> 00:06:41,318
[SPEAKER_03]: I think

66
00:06:42,492 --> 00:06:47,737
[SPEAKER_03]: At this point, we know where our leverage lies, and this is one of the biggest places where it lies.

67
00:06:47,817 --> 00:06:49,478
[SPEAKER_03]: So that would be my proposal.

68
00:06:50,039 --> 00:06:54,342
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I brought forward today, and I look forward to hearing from my fellow counselors about their proposals.

69
00:06:54,442 --> 00:06:54,763
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

70
00:06:55,784 --> 00:06:56,744
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you, Vice President Harris.

71
00:06:57,765 --> 00:07:00,347
[SPEAKER_06]: Anyone else like to speak on what they'd like to see in the budget?

72
00:07:02,669 --> 00:07:03,210
[SPEAKER_06]: Councilor Scarpelli.

73
00:07:04,387 --> 00:07:15,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Madam President, I appreciate this format, and I appreciate my fellow colleagues that put forth such a detailed proposal.

74
00:07:15,217 --> 00:07:27,529
[SPEAKER_01]: My philosophy is that really having this, my thought process is really having this discussion, really talking about it, and I think that, as we did in the past, is sitting and arranging what we

75
00:07:28,677 --> 00:07:31,278
[SPEAKER_01]: we need and what we want as a, as a group.

76
00:07:31,338 --> 00:07:33,759
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that council bears is correct.

77
00:07:33,819 --> 00:07:46,503
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we need to go to the mayor with a fortified front that the seven of us are going to look at certain scenarios with, you know, a no budget mentality that we really need.

78
00:07:46,583 --> 00:07:49,864
[SPEAKER_01]: We, we're not naive to know that we're not

79
00:07:51,014 --> 00:07:53,095
[SPEAKER_01]: shockingly in a budget crunch.

80
00:07:53,695 --> 00:08:14,801
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I've talked to neighboring communities I've, I've talked to state delegates I've talked to, you know, I've talked to our mayor, and for some concerns that we see the long hanging fruit something that I know that the city's looking into right now is making sure that the increasing the fees for our lifeguards to stay competitive

81
00:08:15,608 --> 00:08:23,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Because just last night if you watch the news, the DCR is raising their lifeguard fee per hour to $25 to $26 an hour.

82
00:08:26,476 --> 00:08:29,298
[SPEAKER_01]: they're looking to take hundreds of lifeguards.

83
00:08:29,838 --> 00:08:40,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So what that means is a young boy or girl from Medford is sitting back, that's got their lifeguard certification, that's coming to work for $17 an hour, is thinking, what?

84
00:08:40,744 --> 00:08:44,086
[SPEAKER_01]: I could go to work at Foss Park and make $26 an hour.

85
00:08:44,686 --> 00:08:54,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And what happens is, I don't know if we remember the first year in Mayor Burke's campaign,

86
00:08:55,363 --> 00:09:17,634
[SPEAKER_01]: The biggest issues and concerns that that administration felt they spent more time per day, dealing with issues because of pool issues in pond issues, and it took away so much, so many more important things that the city business had to attack so we're looking at, you know, something small but I think it's, you know,

87
00:09:20,266 --> 00:09:30,409
[SPEAKER_01]: meeting with the recreation director, maybe 30,000 more in a line that would increase their hourly rate, at least not to 25, but something competitive.

88
00:09:31,709 --> 00:09:34,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's not that drastic.

89
00:09:34,130 --> 00:09:46,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that to me is an immediate need that is really going to pay dividends and answer the concerning safety question.

90
00:09:47,395 --> 00:09:48,456
[SPEAKER_01]: at the pond in the pool.

91
00:09:48,896 --> 00:10:04,716
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that that's that to me is to no brainer, I think that we need to, you know, they're, they're the legal representation, and appreciate council bears with the detailed numbers, but I think that is a must I think we, you know, as we debated that.

92
00:10:05,357 --> 00:10:09,080
[SPEAKER_01]: the beginning of this term, we talked about how important that is.

93
00:10:09,821 --> 00:10:11,242
[SPEAKER_01]: And what was it?

94
00:10:11,502 --> 00:10:21,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe a month later, we started, we saw the ramifications with having legal counsel being represented that couldn't represent us because they were in a neighboring community.

95
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:28,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So I think having the assistant city solicitor is something that I will stand with my colleagues with.

96
00:10:28,976 --> 00:10:32,479
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, zoning, that's something that,

97
00:10:34,473 --> 00:10:36,474
[SPEAKER_01]: you see the positive impact right away.

98
00:10:36,514 --> 00:10:42,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think that continuing with the zoning plan that we had in place, I think that funding is important.

99
00:10:42,355 --> 00:10:48,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, low hanging fruit, you know, for what it's worth, it's not a lot of money.

100
00:10:49,177 --> 00:11:01,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, there are positions that I'm curious about that I'm a little nervous about that haven't been filled and knowing where that line item is gone, the facilities manager.

101
00:11:02,987 --> 00:11:14,134
[SPEAKER_01]: From what I hear this concerns at the library, maybe Councilor Caviello can talk about that more later but they don't have anybody qualified to run the library facility, and that's something when we started.

102
00:11:14,915 --> 00:11:19,698
[SPEAKER_01]: We talked about the money that we were invested to library, having somebody that's going to be able to do that.

103
00:11:20,318 --> 00:11:22,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And if we haven't filled that, that's a concern.

104
00:11:24,892 --> 00:11:32,259
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm gonna say it again, one of my priorities is really increasing the numbers of DPW.

105
00:11:32,899 --> 00:11:49,114
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's, we talk about the roads and the sidewalks every day, and the infrastructure problems we have is one of the most important ones, but I really think that we should look into investing in a sidewalk and hot top crew.

106
00:11:49,734 --> 00:12:15,759
[SPEAKER_01]: and adding three more staff for the fact that again we're putting a million dollars in 200 under 250 sidewalk replacements once those are done if we don't have a crew that's continuing that work that we don't have to bond out a million dollars they're only going to start growing again because it's they're just they're deplorable so I think that we really need to have a comprehensive plan in looking at

107
00:12:16,452 --> 00:12:18,433
[SPEAKER_01]: increasing DPW.

108
00:12:18,493 --> 00:12:29,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, not a lot, um, but I'm only, I'm only sharing what over the last year has been pushed and called.

109
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:33,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh, you know, there's other things that, that, you know, we hear every day.

110
00:12:33,882 --> 00:12:40,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Um, you know, education, that's something that the school committee has to deal with right now.

111
00:12:41,026 --> 00:12:42,267
[SPEAKER_01]: I know the mayor is going to give them

112
00:12:43,412 --> 00:12:52,720
[SPEAKER_01]: their number and, but for this side, I think that, you know, I just mentioned just a few things and it's not a lot, but I think we'll pay huge dividends.

113
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:58,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not, and I don't think council bears when he went through his presentation, it wasn't a lot.

114
00:12:58,946 --> 00:13:04,290
[SPEAKER_01]: These are just simple things that are expected in most communities that we're really lacking here.

115
00:13:04,370 --> 00:13:10,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and I'd like to hear from the rest of the team, but I think that these are things that

116
00:13:11,917 --> 00:13:21,720
[SPEAKER_01]: I would support with my fellow colleagues and sitting at budget time, knowing that we need these, a handful of requests that need to be filled.

117
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:23,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So thank you.

118
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:25,661
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you, Councilor Carvalho.

119
00:13:25,861 --> 00:13:27,281
[SPEAKER_06]: Councilor Carvalho and Councilor Collins.

120
00:13:27,341 --> 00:13:28,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Madam President.

121
00:13:28,482 --> 00:13:29,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you for doing this this evening.

122
00:13:30,042 --> 00:13:40,085
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't put anything down formally because as I look over everybody's list here, I mean, is there anything that isn't on here that hasn't been here in years past?

123
00:13:40,888 --> 00:13:42,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's really, it's the same.

124
00:13:42,769 --> 00:13:45,891
[SPEAKER_00]: We keep on asking the same things all over and over and over again.

125
00:13:46,352 --> 00:13:50,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not this mayor, but it's been the mayor before her, the mayor before that.

126
00:13:51,575 --> 00:13:58,480
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, there's nothing really crazy on anyone's wishlist there, but again, it's, you know.

127
00:13:59,368 --> 00:14:03,030
[SPEAKER_00]: For the people, for our newer counselors, these are things we've been asking for.

128
00:14:03,330 --> 00:14:05,131
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been here for my 11th year.

129
00:14:05,752 --> 00:14:07,733
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing here that I haven't asked for.

130
00:14:07,893 --> 00:14:15,137
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been asking for a second code enforcement officer on my very first budget.

131
00:14:16,257 --> 00:14:20,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Three years later, they gave us a half a person, and then he got laid off.

132
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:23,701
[SPEAKER_00]: And we're still doing that.

133
00:14:23,741 --> 00:14:25,582
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the same story every year.

134
00:14:25,622 --> 00:14:26,783
[SPEAKER_00]: We need DPW guys.

135
00:14:28,310 --> 00:14:30,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And the story is everybody knows what we need.

136
00:14:30,811 --> 00:14:36,713
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing here that's earth shattering that should be shaking anybody up.

137
00:14:36,753 --> 00:14:40,354
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'll just fill up on what Councilor Scott Pillay said about the library.

138
00:14:41,234 --> 00:14:44,315
[SPEAKER_00]: We went out and we spent for the state of the art library.

139
00:14:44,355 --> 00:14:47,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we don't have anybody who knows how to operate the state of the art stuff.

140
00:14:48,537 --> 00:14:52,318
[SPEAKER_00]: And the people that are in there now are gonna be out there on June 30th.

141
00:14:53,358 --> 00:14:54,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So we're gonna be kind of on our own.

142
00:14:55,279 --> 00:14:56,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're also complaining about,

143
00:14:59,488 --> 00:15:03,989
[SPEAKER_00]: That's too big of a building not to be cleaned every day.

144
00:15:04,409 --> 00:15:05,450
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not a three day a week.

145
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:09,651
[SPEAKER_00]: There's 15,000 people a week going through that place.

146
00:15:09,731 --> 00:15:11,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't clean it every other day.

147
00:15:11,251 --> 00:15:11,911
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too dirty.

148
00:15:12,232 --> 00:15:19,734
[SPEAKER_00]: That needs its own... It's a two story building that has to be cleaned every single day and the grounds.

149
00:15:21,554 --> 00:15:23,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Are we gonna do like we do with all our other buildings?

150
00:15:23,835 --> 00:15:24,595
[SPEAKER_00]: We build them and then...

151
00:15:25,797 --> 00:15:28,159
[SPEAKER_00]: just let them go away and don't spend anything.

152
00:15:28,179 --> 00:15:32,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's been our MO in the city for the past 30 years.

153
00:15:33,684 --> 00:15:37,768
[SPEAKER_00]: We fix it, when the ashtray gets filled, we throw it out and buy a new car.

154
00:15:38,909 --> 00:15:42,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's been the model of past groups.

155
00:15:42,792 --> 00:15:48,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So again, I thank my fellow colleagues for putting on everything that's, everything here is glaring.

156
00:15:50,109 --> 00:15:52,913
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing here that anybody asked for that's crazy.

157
00:15:53,514 --> 00:15:59,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, we ask for the same things every year and every year we're back here doing the exact same thing we're doing now.

158
00:16:00,626 --> 00:16:03,191
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you, Councilor Carvillo, Councilor Collins, and then Councilor Sanz.

159
00:16:03,830 --> 00:16:04,610
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, President Morrell.

160
00:16:05,491 --> 00:16:09,713
[SPEAKER_05]: I mainly just want to emphasize a few things that I've already heard, and I agree.

161
00:16:09,773 --> 00:16:15,136
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that there's largely broad consensus in some of our key priorities.

162
00:16:15,996 --> 00:16:20,999
[SPEAKER_05]: I appreciate the opportunity, you know, the prompt to jot down some priorities prior to this meeting.

163
00:16:21,579 --> 00:16:33,585
[SPEAKER_05]: I wrote down many, many bullet points, but I think really just the first three or four are the real short-term priorities that I wanted to speak to, and the rest I wanted to enter into the record

164
00:16:35,186 --> 00:16:46,599
[SPEAKER_05]: longer term priorities kind of to bolster the point that I think that we've been making repeatedly for weeks, if not for the entire term and before that as well, of the need to get to a place where we can more sustainably and more

165
00:16:47,620 --> 00:17:17,022
[SPEAKER_05]: effectively fund more of the services and status quo that our constituents really need and deserve, such as accelerating the timeline for pavement and sidewalk repair through whatever means, increasing our DPW staff, et cetera, and many other projects besides that we already have articulated priorities on, whether it's things that are in the draft comprehensive plan, like getting to more net zero municipal buildings, expanding our biking infrastructure, all priorities that this administration has already articulated, and I have no idea

